        "note": "'This spacious structure had just been completed at an expense of $80,000 ... The building was the finest of its kind in the country' (Andreas vol. 2, p. 719). Its walls stood after the fire (the fire map's fate). Its size is reconstructed on the brick stable Parmelee built in its place within eleven weeks, a hundred by a hundred and fifty-three feet (Andreas vol. 2, pp. 118-119); its three floors (stalls and carriages, more stalls, the hay loft), round-arched carriage doors, high stall windows, loft hay doors and ventilators are reconstructed as a large city livery and transfer stable of 1871."
